No. Zeros in language are not expressed unconciously. They're conciously 
unexpressed. They're omitted because they're obvious from context or 
irrelevant. When I say "I saw a girl with a telescope", it's not obvious 
that I really mean "I saw a girl and either I used telescope to see her or 
she had a telescope with her". In fact I probably don't. The result of 
parsing should show what was meant and not what are all of the possible and 
probably not intended syntactic constructions in the sentence.

On the other hand, when I say in Polish "Jem" (I eat) and not "Ja jem" (I 
eat), I'm very concious that "Ja" (I) is the subject, this follows from the 
form of the verb (first person). It's not like I unconciously express that 
I'm talking about me and I'd be surprised when I find out (which is often 
true with syntactic ambiguity). The same goes for the examples in the 
Wikipedia article.

You could argue that in "X of Y of Z" there are zero parenthesis-like 
tokens: "( X of Y ) of Z" or "X of (Y of Z)". But it doesn't help with 
parsing, it


